/E (The core execution divergence is implemented utilizing Python's native `threading.Thread` module. When the script's `__main__` block executes, it immediately spawns a secondary worker thread via the syntax: `threading.Thread\(target=bot_loop, daemon=True\).start\(\)`. The explicit injection of the `daemon=True` parameter is a highly critical architectural requirement for the system's stability.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
175 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (Following successful health verification \(or subsequent self-healing\), the `run_retail_automation\(\)` function generates bounded random floating-point values to simulate a transaction. A localized `sqlite3` cursor is then securely opened to execute parameterized `INSERT INTO` statements, followed immediately by a hard `conn.commit\(\)`. Following the database lock release, the `socketio.emit\(\)` method mathematically serializes the updated global dictionary state and pushes it to the client over the TCP socket. Finally, `focus_window\(\)` invokes the OS API to locate the target application, followed by blocking `pyautogui.write\(\)` operations.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (Setting the thread as a daemon ensures that if the primary Flask server thread encounters a fatal exception, or if a system administrator issues a manual SIGINT \(CTRL+C\) command to the terminal, the background automation loop is immediately and forcefully terminated by the Python interpreter. Without this flag, the infinite `while` loop would survive the death of the parent process, resulting in an unkillable zombie process that would continuously inject ghost keystrokes into the host operating system, severely compromising the machine.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
