/E (The system requires incredibly robust exception handling when interfacing with volatile external networks and restrictive hardware I/O boundaries. The outbound logic must be defensive, assuming that the OS or the network will eventually reject a request.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (The system incorporates a framework for outbound alerting, designed to dispatch HTTP POST payloads to a remote server webhook \(e.g., Discord or Slack\) to provide critical failure notifications to human engineers. Recognizing that external edge networks are inherently volatile and subject to DNS failures, packet loss, or timeout limits, any `requests.post\(\)` calls are entirely wrapped in bare `try...except:` blocks.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (This explicit error handling is a critical architectural decision. If the host machine drops its Wi-Fi connection, or the remote API experiences downtime, the resulting `ConnectionError` is silently trapped and logged locally. This allows the retail automation bot to bypass the network failure and continue processing physical POS transactions uninterrupted. Without this failsafe, a simple network dropout would fatally crash the entire monitoring daemon.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 108 0 R
/E (For hardware interfaces, the `pyautogui` library interacts directly with the OS peripheral drivers to simulate a cashier. A `pyautogui.FailSafeException` \(triggered if the user throws the mouse to the corner of the screen\) is mitigated by artificially injecting strict `time.sleep\(\)` commands between keystrokes. This prevents buffer overruns during the physical receipt typing phase, ensuring the Windows Desktop Manager has sufficient CPU cycles to actually render the text before the next key is struck.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
