<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (State mutability is highly localized within the script to prevent memory leaks and variable corruption across the parallel threads. The global `web_data` dictionary explicitly defines variable types upon initial script instantiation \(e.g., initializing integers exactly as `0`, floats specifically as `0.0`, and strings as `"--"`\). )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (This rigid, preemptive definition prevents critical type-coercion errors during the automatic JSON serialization phase executed by the Flask-SocketIO emitter. If a float was accidentally passed as a string, it could fatally crash the frontend DOM rendering logic in Chart.js.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
250 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (Within the local `run_retail_automation\(\)` scope, the script uses the explicit Python `global web_data` directive to authorize the in-place mutation of these existing memory keys \(e.g., executing `web_data["total_revenue"] += total`\). This exact operational pattern maintains a continuous, stateful application session directly within the Python runtime RAM. By utilizing active RAM as the definitive, high- speed state cache, the script completely bypasses the need for repetitive, latency- heavy database `SELECT` polling to update the frontend dashboard, vastly accelerating execution cycles and reducing disk wear.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
